



:: day_3_1_other
<<set $day to 3>>
I wake up and the first thing I do is check the dashboard.

Not my phone. Not the time. Not the ceiling crack, which is still there, still growing, still splitting the plaster in its patient, geological way. The dashboard. I roll off the mattress, cross the two steps to the desk in my boxers and the same grey shirt I slept in, jiggle the mouse, and watch the second monitor wake with the ElliePlayz stats from last night still displayed like a stock ticker from a dream I can't believe I had.

Peak Viewers: 89
Average Viewers: 61
New Followers: 47
Donations: $47.00
Chat Messages: 312

It's real. I stare at the numbers the way I used to stare at the ceiling crack   with a fixed, trance-like attention that borders on devotion   except these numbers don't fill me with dread. They fill me with something else. Something that vibrates in my fingertips and my jawline and the pit of my stomach. Something I haven't felt since I first started streaming eight months ago, back when I still believed the next stream would be the one that changed everything.

Hope. Except this time the hope has evidence.

$47 in one hour. As a voice. As a name. As a pink-and-purple gradient with a cursive font. 47 followers in one session   that's 87% of what EliPlays accumulated over eight entire months. In sixty minutes, Ellie eclipsed Eli.

I sit in the desk chair. It sinks its customary inch and a half. The hydraulic wheezes. The apartment is the same   cold, dim, the fridge humming, the burrito wrappers breeding on the counter   but the numbers on the screen are a window into a different apartment, a different life, a different version of this same morning where the person sitting in this chair isn't calculating how many days until the $47 in his bank account runs out but calculating how quickly the $47 on his dashboard can multiply.

My phone buzzes. I grab it off the floor.

<<kira "hey. hope you got home safe last night.">>

Kira. The message from last night   the one I answered with "night kira" and no heart, the bare two words, the closed door. She sent this at 8:15 AM. It's now 9:47. She's been awake for an hour and a half and the first thing she did was check on me.

The warmth is there. The Kira warmth. Even in the standard route, even in the version where I laughed off calling her hot and killed the moment with cowardice, the warmth is there   muted, banked, a fire I covered with ashes but didn't extinguish. She still cares. She still sends red hearts. She still asks if I got home safe.

<<eli "yeah got home fine. late night">>

<<kira "streaming?">>

<<eli "yeah. working on some stuff">>

<<kira "the mysterious 'stuff.' one day you'll tell me what that means">>

<<eli "one day">>

<<kira "🙄 anyway. stream at noon if you want to watch. doing a Just Chatting Q&A today">>

<<eli "might tune in">>

<<kira "you better. i'm answering embarrassing questions from chat and i need moral support">>

<<eli "i'll be there">>

<<kira "❤️">>

Red heart. She's still sending them. Even after the laugh. Even after I closed the door. Kira Nomura sends red hearts the way the sun produces light   not because it's asked to, not because it's reciprocated, but because that's what it does. It's her nature. The warmth radiates outward regardless of whether it's absorbed.

I don't send one back. I send a thumbs up. The forty-year-old dad emoji. The emotional equivalent of a firm handshake when someone opens their arms for a hug.

I put the phone down. Look at the dashboard. Look at the phone. Dashboard. Phone. Two realities, two screens, two versions of who I am and what I want.

The dashboard wins.

I open the browser. Tab one: the ElliePlayz channel page. 47 followers. The number is small but it's real and it's growing and the growth happened in an hour and the trajectory, if it holds, projects into numbers that make my stomach flip. Tab two: YouTube. The VoxMorph tutorials I bookmarked last night. Tab three, which I open now with the deliberate, focused energy of a person who has identified a problem and is solving it:

Google search: "how to look feminine fast"

[[How to look feminine fast|day_3_2_other]]

:: day_3_2_other
<<set $hint to "Go down the rabbit hole">>
<<set $day_step to 2>>
The rabbit hole opens.

It starts with makeup tutorials. Dozens of them. YouTube is overflowing with videos titled "FEMININE MAKEUP FOR BEGINNERS" and "MTF MAKEUP TRANSFORMATION" and "How to Feminize Your Face in 10 Minutes." I watch three of them in succession   women and trans women and drag queens demonstrating contouring, highlighting, the strategic application of color to reshape the way light hits bone. The techniques are impressive but impractical for my purposes: makeup is visible on camera, especially on a webcam with bad resolution, and the time investment is significant. Twenty minutes of contouring before every stream, plus the risk of smudging, sweating, the makeup shifting mid-session.

I want something more permanent. Something that doesn't wash off.

There's the obvious solution. The one any tech-literate person in 2027 would think of first.

AI face-swap. Real-time deepfake filters. The technology exists   apps that map a generated face onto yours live, rendering a completely synthetic person over your webcam feed. Half the internet runs on some version of it.

I consider it for exactly twelve minutes.

The problem isn't quality   for pre-recorded content, AI faces are nearly flawless. A YouTube video, a TikTok, an edited clip   you could run a generated face through post-processing and nobody would ever know. But I'm not making videos. I'm live streaming. Real-time. Two hours of continuous webcam, every frame rendered on the fly, every expression tracked and mapped and composited at 30fps with zero room for error and the errors come. They always come.

There was a streamer   IndigoVeil   who ran a real-time AI face filter for four months. Premium GPU, calibrated lighting, the best software money could buy. It worked beautifully. Until she sneezed. One involuntary full-body sneeze that the motion-tracking couldn't follow. The AI lost the face for less than a second. One frame of the real face underneath   wrong jaw, wrong skin, wrong everything. One viewer screenshotted it. Reddit post. Viral in six hours. Clip channels dissecting it frame by frame. 2,000 viewers became zero overnight. Account deleted within 48 hours.

That's the premium version. I have a 2014 webcam and a thrift-store desk lamp. The composite would bleed at the hairline within minutes. The lip sync would lag on every third sentence. The micro-expressions   the wide-eyed gasp, the slow blink, the thinking face   would arrive 40 milliseconds behind the voice, and the regulars who watch every stream would clock it in a week.

Pre-made content? AI is perfect for it. Live streaming? It's a time bomb with a sneeze for a trigger and when it detonates   one screenshot, one Reddit post, one clip channel   there's no recovery. The internet doesn't forgive fake faces. It archives them.

I close the FaceMorph tab. AI is the last resort. The nuclear option I'd only touch if nothing else worked, and even then I'd be building on sand.

The voice modulator is a different story. VoxMorph works   it works well enough for now. The pitch shift is clean, the formant adjustment is natural, and through Twitch compression at 128kbps audio nobody can tell the difference between processed and organic. It's reliable in a way that AI face-swap isn't. A voice doesn't sneeze. A voice doesn't glitch at the hairline. The processing is consistent, frame after frame, hour after hour.

But "well enough for now" isn't "permanent." I can hear the seams if I listen for them   a faint digital shimmer on certain vowels, a flatness in the laugh that a real feminine voice wouldn't have, the way the software rounds off the edges of my natural register instead of truly reshaping them. Chat doesn't notice. Chat is listening through laptop speakers and earbuds and the general audio soup of a Twitch stream. But someone with good headphones and an ear for audio? A sound engineer? A voice coach? They'd hear the processing. They'd hear the machine inside the girl.

There's the fatigue. Two hours of speaking through VoxMorph strains my throat differently than normal talking   I'm pitching my natural voice into a range that the software can work with, staying in a narrow band that processes cleanly, avoiding the low register that makes the algorithm stutter. By the end of a long session my vocal cords ache in a way that feels unsustainable. I can't do this for years. The software is a bridge, not a destination.

RegenaForm's menu listed voice modulation   organic, not software. Tier 2. Actual restructuring of the vocal cords and resonance chambers. A voice that's mine, that's feminine, that doesn't need a plugin. That doesn't fatigue. That doesn't shimmer on certain vowels.

But that's later. That's Tier 2. That's thousands of Dollars I don't have yet.

For now, VoxMorph holds. The voice is the most reliable part of the illusion   more reliable than the crop-line framing, more reliable than the wig, infinitely more reliable than any AI face would be. It's enough. For now.

The face is the problem. The face needs to be real.

I need something real. Something that IS the face, not something projected onto it. Something a viewer can screenshot and frame-by-frame and zoom into and find nothing wrong   because the lashes are actual lashes and the brows are actual brows and every cell is mine.


The search mutates. "How to look feminine without makeup." "Facial feminization non-surgical." "Subtle feminization techniques for streaming." The results shift from YouTube to forums   Reddit threads, niche communities, spaces where people discuss passing and presentation and the specific art of looking like someone other than who you were born as.

I read for forty-five minutes. The information is dense, detailed, and organized by the kind of people who have spent years thinking about exactly this problem. Brow shaping. Eyelash extensions. Skincare routines that change the texture and luminosity of skin. Hair   real hair, wig options, styling techniques. Each topic is a door, and behind each door is a room full of more doors, and the architecture of the rabbit hole is designed to keep you descending and then   on the second page of a Google search for "facial feminization fast results"   the ad.

It doesn't look like an ad. It looks like a search result, clean and authoritative, with a thumbnail image of a chrome double helix and text that reads:

RegenaForm   Unlock Your True Form DNA-based bioengineering. No scars. Full sensory integration. Consultations available.

I click it.

The website loads and it's   beautiful. In the way that a luxury car showroom is beautiful, or a five-star hotel lobby. The design communicates money and competence and the specific brand of confidence that comes from knowing your product works. White space. Clean typography. The chrome double-helix logo rotating slowly in the header. A tagline beneath it: "We don't change you. We unlock you."

I scroll. Before-and-after gallery. Each transformation shown in high resolution, the images so detailed they look medical and so beautiful they look editorial. Faces shifting from one configuration to another   masculine to feminine, angular to soft, forgettable to magnetic. The changes range from subtle (a jawline refined, skin cleared) to dramatic (full facial feminization, the bone structure literally reshaped, a different person emerging from beneath the same skin).

Services. Listed in tiers. Tier 1: Facial Feminization. Eyelashes, brow sculpting, nose refinement, jawline contouring, lip enhancement, skin treatments. Tier 2: Voice Modulation (organic   not software). Tier 3: Hair. Tier 4: Body contour. Tiers 5 through 8: escalating, invasive, transformative. The prices are in Dollars, a cryptocurrency I've seen in niche forums. The conversion rate to USD is roughly 1:1.

Tier 1 starts at 500 $. Individual features are cheaper. Eyelash enhancement: 200-350 $. Brow sculpting: 300-500 $. The numbers are large for someone with $47 in the bank but small for someone who just earned $47 in an hour.

I read the FAQ.

"Is the process reversible?"   "All modifications can be revised or reversed. See our Revision Policy."

"Does it hurt?"   "Our DNA-based technology integrates with your own genetic material. Most clients describe the sensation as 'warm' or 'tingling.' No anesthesia required for Tier 1."

"How long does it take?"   "Tier 1 procedures range from 20 minutes to 2 hours. Results are immediate."

Immediate. No surgery. No scarring. No downtime. I read the word "immediate" three times and each time it sounds more like a promise and less like a claim.

At the bottom of the FAQ, a button: Book Free Consultation.

I click it before the rational part of my brain can file an objection. The form loads. Name: Ellie. Email: ellieplayz.stream@gmail.com (created on the spot, fingers moving faster than thought). Phone: my real number. Date: today. Time: the dropdown shows 2:30 PM available.

I submit. Confirmation page: "Thank you, Ellie! Your consultation with Dr. Voss is confirmed for 2:30 PM today. 1440 Meridian Ave, Suite 12."

1440 Meridian. Four blocks from my apartment. The clinic that promises to unlock my "true form" is a fifteen-minute walk from the building where I've been slowly decomposing for eight months. The proximity feels like fate or marketing. Probably marketing.

I close the browser. Open it. Close it. Open it one more time, look at the confirmation, and close it for real.

It's 11:30 AM. Three hours until the consultation.

I should stream. I should open EliPlays and grind ranked and talk to DarkSlayer and earn my $1.75 per hour and maintain the fiction that the first account still matters. But the ElliePlayz dashboard is glowing on the second monitor and the $47 is still there and the idea of going live as Eli right now feels like putting on a costume   the wrong costume, the one that doesn't fit, the one the audience doesn't want to see.

I eat a microwave burrito. Rotate at one minute. Chicken and cheese. Warm salt and texture. Monster Energy, the last one. The green can, original flavor. Breakfast of a person whose diet will kill him before the streaming does.

I watch Kira's stream for an hour. She's doing the Q&A she mentioned   sitting in front of the ring light, the bob perfect, the glasses catching the LED glow, answering questions from chat with the easy charisma of someone who's done this a thousand times. Her viewer count hovers around 950. The donations scroll. I watch her talk and laugh and perform and I feel the familiar two-part feeling: the warmth (she's incredible, she's magnetic, her laugh does things to my heartbeat) and the math (950 viewers, $12 in donations in the ten minutes I've been watching, $72 an hour, $288 in four hours, and I have $47 in the bank).

She says something that makes me sit up.

A viewer asks: "What advice would you give to a new streamer trying to grow?"

<<kira "Honestly? Be authentic. Find the thing that makes you you and lean into it. The algorithm rewards consistency and personality. Don't try to be someone you're not   the audience can tell.">>

Don't try to be someone you're not.

I close her stream. I can't watch right now. The cognitive dissonance is giving me a headache   the girl I almost kissed yesterday telling me to be authentic while I'm sitting at a desk with a voice modulator installed and a consultation booked at a bioengineering clinic to make my face look like someone else's.

12:30 PM. Two hours until RegenaForm.

I shower. Use the Marriott shampoo. Not for Kira this time   for the consultation. For Dr. Voss, whoever they are. I want to look... presentable. Not feminine, not yet, just like a person who cares about their appearance, who takes this seriously, who isn't walking in off the street in a state of visible desperation.

I put on the grey shirt. Black joggers. The hoodie. The crushed sneakers. I look in the phone camera. The face is the same   pale, dark circles, thin lips, greasy hair that the shampoo improved but didn't transform. The face that earned $3.50. The face I'm about to ask a stranger to change.

I leave the apartment at 2:10. The walk takes twelve minutes.

<<link "Go to the consultation">><<set $hint to "Go to the consultation at Regeneform">><<goto neighbourhood>><</link>>


:: day_3_3_other

Suite 12. Second floor. A frosted glass door with a chrome double helix etched into it, catching the hallway's fluorescent light and turning it into something elegant. No signage. No description. Just the logo and the suite number and the quiet implication that if you're here, you already know what this is.

I push the door open.

The waiting area is designed to make you forget you're in a building on Meridian Ave that also houses an accountant and a bail bondsman. Hardwood floors, polished. Two dove-grey couches with angular precision. A glass coffee table with a single orchid and a stack of lookbooks   glossy, heavy-stock portfolios of before-and-after transformations that sit between the orchid and a small bowl of individually wrapped mints like an art exhibition catalog at the world's most unsettling gallery. The lighting is warm, recessed, the kind that makes everyone look better, which is either thoughtful design or a cruel preview of services available and then there's the receptionist.

Lina.

I've seen attractive people before. I live in a city. I browse the internet. I have functioning eyes. But Lina exists at a frequency of beauty that makes the word "attractive" feel like bringing a butter knife to a sword fight. She's standing behind the reception desk in a fitted dove-grey dress with the chrome helix embroidered on the chest, and everything about her   the honey-toned skin, the doe-shaped amber-gold eyes, the caramel-highlighted waves, the lips in a permanent, precise smile   reads as an answer to a question nobody asked. What does "perfect" look like? This. Apparently.

The thing that gets me   the thing that hooks into my brain and won't let go   is that I can't tell what she is. Ethnically, genetically, categorically. She could be anything. Everything. The universality of her beauty is its own uncanny valley   too balanced, too symmetrical, too carefully composed, like a face designed by a committee whose only mandate was "no flaws."

She's a walking advertisement. RegenaForm's best pitch isn't on the website or in the lookbooks. It's standing behind the desk, smiling, being impossible.

<<lina "Welcome to RegenaForm! You must be Ellie.">>

The voice is melodic, warm, modulated. Every word placed like a brushstroke.

<<eli "Yeah. Hi.">>

My voice sounds like gravel in comparison. Male, flat, the voice that earned $3.50. Standing in front of Lina, I'm acutely aware of every masculine thing about myself   the jaw, the Adam's apple, the broad knuckles, the shoes, the posture. I'm a rough sketch standing in front of a finished painting.

<<lina "Wonderful. Dr. Voss will be right with you. Can I get you anything? Water, tea, sparkling?">>

<<eli "No, I'm good. Thanks.">>

<<lina "Of course. Please make yourself comfortable.">>

I sit on the grey couch. It's beautiful and rigid. I pick up a lookbook and flip through it   transformation after transformation, each spread a before-and-after that challenges the concept of identity. A jaw goes from square to soft. A brow ridge flattens. Skin clears from acne-scarred to porcelain. Each page is a person becoming a different person, or the same person becoming more of themselves, depending on which story you want to tell.

I'm on a spread showing a full facial feminization   a face I would have read as unmistakably male on the left page and unmistakably female on the right   when the back door opens.

[[Doctor arrives|day_3_4_other]]

:: day_3_4_other
<<set $hint to "Meet Doctor Maren Voss">>
Dr. Maren Voss.

Tall. Lean. Mid-thirties. A face that refuses categorization   sharp cheekbones and soft lips, strong brow and arched eyebrows, the kind of face that makes your brain try to assign a gender and fail and try again and fail again and eventually give up. Ash-blonde hair pulled sleekly back. Cool grey-green eyes that look at you the way a scanner looks at a barcode   not judgmentally, not warmly, just completely. White lab coat. Black high-collared shirt. The RegenaForm helix pin on the lapel.

<<dr_maren "Ellie. I'm Dr. Maren Voss. Please, come in.">>

I follow them through the door. The consultation room is behind it   warm lighting, two chairs facing each other across a low table, a large wall-mounted screen. More design studio than medical office. The whole clinic is like this   aggressively not-clinical, as if the aesthetic itself is part of the treatment, the first modification being your assumption of what this experience would feel like.

I sit. Dr. Maren sits across from me. The grey-green eyes settle on my face with the attention of someone beginning an inventory.

<<dr_maren "Tell me what brings you here.">>

I've rehearsed this. Multiple versions. The one I deliver is the one that sounds most like a normal person making a normal request:

<<eli "I'm a content creator. Streamer. I want to look better on camera. More... polished. Maybe a little more feminine. Not a full transformation   just enough to be camera-friendly.">>

Dr. Maren listens without expression. The neutral attention of a professional who has heard this before   this specific request, this specific framing, this specific dance between what the client is saying and what the client means.

<<dr_maren "Camera-friendly. Can you elaborate?">>

<<eli "My face doesn't... it doesn't stop people. When they see my thumbnail, they scroll past. I need a face that makes people click.">>

<<dr_maren "You want a face that performs.">>

<<eli "...yeah. A face that performs.">>

<<dr_maren "May I?">>

They gesture toward my face. I nod. They lean forward and take my chin between two fingers   cool, dry, impersonal   and turn my head. Left. Right. Center. Up. Down. The examination is brief and thorough, the way a mechanic examines an engine   checking components, identifying the parts that work and the parts that need replacing.

<<dr_maren "Interesting bone structure underneath the neglect. The jaw has feminine potential   it's not heavily set, just undefined. The brow ridge is moderate. Skin quality is poor but treatable. Eye shape is actually quite good   balanced, proportional. The main masculine reads are the brow, the nose, and the skin texture. Everything else is workable.">>

They release my chin. Turn to the wall screen. A tap on a hidden tablet and my face appears   captured by a camera I didn't notice, displayed in merciless high resolution under clinical lighting. Every pore. Every blemish. Every asymmetry. I look like an evidence photo of my own mediocrity.

A digital overlay appears   architectural lines mapping the bone structure, highlighting the masculine markers in red and the neutral/feminine zones in blue.

<<dr_maren "Here's what I see.">>

They trace the red zones.

<<dr_maren "Brow ridge   moderately heavy. Reduces the vertical space of the eye area, codes masculine. Nose   wide bridge, rounded tip, unremarkable but distinctly male. Jaw   could go either way with treatment. Skin   acne, texture issues, dullness. These are the barriers between your current face and a face that... performs.">>

They tap the screen. The overlay shifts. The red zones morph   the brow softens, the lashes lengthen, the skin smooths. A predictive rendering of the same face with Tier 1 modifications applied. The face on screen is still mine but also not mine   it's the face I'd have if I'd been born with different settings, if the genetic sliders had been positioned two notches to the left on every axis.

<<dr_maren "This is achievable. Tier 1 only. No structural bone work. DNA-based modifications using your own genetic material   no rejection, no scarring, full sensory preservation. The technology activates dormant potential in your existing cells. We're not adding anything foreign. We're unlocking what's already there.">>

Unlocking what's already there. The tagline from the website, delivered in person, with the weight of a doctor's authority and the precision of a sales pitch. I can't tell where the medicine ends and the marketing begins. Maybe there's no line.

<<eli "What would you recommend? To start?">>

<<dr_maren "For a first visit, we typically recommend eyelash enhancement   it's our most popular introductory procedure and we offer it complimentary for new clients.">>

Free. The first one's free.

<<dr_maren "Natural-Long eyelashes. Dense, dark, naturally long. Subtle enough that casual acquaintances won't identify the change consciously, but the effect on camera is dramatic. Lashes frame the eyes. Framed eyes hold attention.">>

<<eli "And beyond that?">>

<<dr_maren "If you'd like to invest in one paid procedure today, the brow sculpt. Feathered   soft, natural-looking, feminine arch. Three hundred Dollars. Opens the eye area, reduces the masculine coding of the brow ridge. Combined with the lashes, the effect is... transformative might be too strong a word. Let's say: significant.">>

300 $. $300. I don't have $300. I have $47 in the bank plus $47 on the ElliePlayz dashboard that hasn't hit my account yet. But the $47 from last night will clear. If I stream tonight   which I will, which I'm already planning, the stream already taking shape in the back of my mind   the numbers suggest another $40-50 minimum. In two days, the brow sculpt pays for itself.

<<eli "Payment plan?">>

<<dr_maren "We offer credit. Dollars-based. Seven-day payment window. The eyelashes are complimentary. The brow sculpt   300 $   billed to your streaming account.">>

<<eli "My streaming account?">>

<<dr_maren "ElliePlayz.">>

I don't ask how they know. I should ask how they know. A doctor at a clinic I found on Google two hours ago just said my fake streaming account name   the one I created last night at midnight, the one with 47 followers, the one that exists in a pocket of the internet so small you'd need a microscope to find it   and I should be alarmed by this. I should feel the hair on my neck stand up and the alarm bells ring and the rational part of my brain should be screaming how do they know, who told them, what are they tracking.

But the $47 is on the dashboard and the rendering of my face on the screen looks like a person people would click on and Dr. Maren's grey-green eyes are steady and the word "complimentary" is still hanging in the air like a gift, and I don't ask. I cross the bridge without looking down.

<<eli "How did you ">>

<<dr_maren "We keep our ear to the ground. New streamers with... potential... tend to find their way here. It's not unusual. Many of our most successful clients began exactly where you are.">>

They say it smoothly. Too smoothly. The words are a bridge over a question I should be asking   how do you know my stream name?   but the bridge is well-built and the far side has a brow sculpt and eyelashes waiting, and I cross it without looking down.

<<eli "Okay. Let's do it. Eyelashes and brow sculpt.">>

<<dr_maren "For streamers, we also include a complimentary professional wig from our StreamLine series. Camera-optimized. Natural appearance. Consider it a welcome gift.">>

Three modifications. Two free. One on credit. The math of the deal is so favorable it should be suspicious, and it is suspicious, and I take it anyway because the rendering on the screen shows a face that could stream without hiding behind a nose-down crop and the numbers say that face would be worth more than $47 an hour.

<<dr_maren "Excellent. Follow me.">>

[[Follow Dr. Maren to the procedure room|day_3_5_other]]

:: day_3_5_other
<<set $hint to "The procedure">>

The procedure room. Reclining chair. Adjustable lights. A tray of instruments that look less like medical tools and more like the contents of a very expensive artist's kit. The screen on the wall shows my face with the target areas highlighted   lashes in blue, brows in green.

I lie back. The chair reclines silently   no wheezing, no hydraulic death rattle, just smooth mechanical precision. The ceiling above is white and featureless. No crack. The absence feels wrong, like a face without features.

<<dr_maren "Eyelash enhancement first. Twenty minutes. You'll feel warmth at the application site. Close your eyes.">>

I close them. A cool gel touches my eyelids. Then warmth   spreading, gradual, not unpleasant. Like sunlight through a window, concentrated on the tissue around my eyes. I can feel activity   not movement, not growth in the way you'd imagine growth, but a stirring. Something cellular being switched on. Dormant potential being activated. The science or the poetry of it   I can't tell which and I'm not sure it matters.

Twenty minutes. I lie there with my eyes closed and I think about the numbers.

$47 per hour × 3 hours per day × 7 days = $987 per week.
$987 per week × 4 weeks = $3,948 per month.

Four thousand dollars a month. Streaming as Ellie. That's rent plus groceries plus utilities plus enough left over to start climbing the transformation tiers, to invest back into the face, to make the face better which makes the numbers higher which makes the face better which makes the numbers higher 

The loop is already running. I can feel it   the feedback circuit, the escalation logic, the same compulsive optimization that Kira described when she talked about her content strategy except pointed inward. At myself. At my face. The product and the marketing are the same thing and the thing they're selling is me and the buyer is the internet and the currency is attention.

<<dr_maren "Eyelashes complete. Open your eyes.">>

[[Open eyes after eyelash procedure|day_3_6_other]]

:: day_3_6_other
<<set $hint to "Open your eyes">>
I open them. Mirror. My face. The same face, mostly. But the eyes 
<<set $appearance   to 3>>
<<set $femininity   to 1>>
<<set $playerImage = "images/user/tier_0_1.jpeg">>
<img src="images/user/tier_0_1.jpeg" alt="Eli's face after eyelash enhancement" class="scene-image">
The lashes. Long, dark, dense, fanning out from my eyelids like the bristles of a brush dipped in ink. They catch the clinical light and cast micro-shadows on my cheekbones, tiny half-moons of shade that didn't exist twenty minutes ago. The eyes themselves are the same   brown, tired, the dark circles still visible   but the framing has changed. The lashes create a border, a declaration, a reason for a viewer's gaze to pause.

<<dr_maren "Natural-Long. Dense, dark. No maintenance. They'll grow at the same rate as your natural lashes. The length is calibrated for standard webcam distance   one to three feet.">>

Calibrated. For webcam distance. They've thought about this. They've optimized the lash length for the specific use case of being seen through a camera. The product is designed for the market. The market is me.

<<dr_maren "Now the brows. Thirty minutes. Same process.">>

The brow procedure involves more shaping   Dr. Maren's fingers trace the new arch with the precision of someone drawing a line they've drawn a thousand times, mapping the geometry of feminization onto the canvas of my brow ridge. The warmth spreads across my forehead, concentrated at the brow line, and I feel the restructuring   the cells shifting, reorganizing, the thick masculine arch thinning and lifting into something feathered and soft.

Thirty minutes. Mirror again.

[[See the new brows|day_3_7_other]]

:: day_3_7_other
<<set $hint to "See the new brows">>

The brows have changed. Not dramatically   not yet. Dr. Maren says they'll continue refining for two to three hours, the full shape emerging gradually. But already the difference is visible. The thick, low-set, slightly unruly brows I've had my whole life are thinner now, with a soft arch where there was none, a curve that lifts the eye area upward and creates the impression of more space, more openness, more approachability. The combination of lashes and brows makes my forehead look different   less heavy, less masculine, lighter in the way that Dr. Maren described.
<<set $appearance   to 4>>
<<set $femininity   to 2>>
<<set $playerImage = "images/user/tier_0_2.jpeg">>
<img src="images/user/tier_0_2.jpeg" alt="Eli's face after eyelash enhancement" class="scene-image">

I look at my face. The face with the new lashes and the reshaping brows and the same acne and dark circles and thin lips. It's a face in transition   not between genders, not yet, but between states. Between the face that earns $3.50 and the face that might earn more. Between EliPlays and ElliePlayz. Between the person I was yesterday and the person I'm deciding to become.

<<dr_maren "The brows will reach their final shape by this evening. By tomorrow, you won't remember what the previous ones looked like.">>

[[Wig fitting with Lina|day_3_8_other]]

:: day_3_8_other
<<set $hint to "Wig fitting with Lina">>

Lina fits the wig. A side room   vanity mirror, lit, a wall of wig forms. She selects one without asking my preference, as if the choice was made before I arrived.

<<lina "StreamLine series. Medium-length, soft waves, warm brunette. The fiber is camera-optimized   responds to light the way biological hair does, no synthetic sheen. The cap is adjustable, the lace front invisible.">>

She puts it on me. Tucks my hair. Adjusts the cap. Aligns the lace. Her hands are efficient and warm and smell like vanilla and something that might be fabric softener or might be the ambient scent of someone who was manufactured to be perfect.

<<lina "There.">>

[[Look in the mirror with the new wig|day_3_9_other]]

:: day_3_9_other
<<set $hint to "Look in the mirror with the new wig">>
<<set $loan        to 300>>
She turns me to the mirror. The person looking back at me is 

Not me. Except it is me. Every feature, individually, is mine   the eyes, the nose, the mouth, the skin. But the sum has changed. The wig   warm brunette waves falling to the shoulders, soft and real-looking and catching the vanity light like hair in a shampoo ad   reframes the entire face. The lashes anchor the eyes. The reshaping brows lift the forehead. The hair completes the illusion that isn't quite an illusion because it's built from my own cells, my own genetics, my own "dormant potential" switched on by a clinic four blocks from my apartment.

<<set $playerImage = "images/user/tier_0_3.jpeg">>
<img src="images/user/tier_0_3.jpeg" alt="Eli's face after eyelash enhancement" class="scene-image">

I don't look female. I look... ambiguous. From certain angles, in certain light, the face could go either way. The jaw is still too angular, the Adam's apple still visible, the skin still rough. But from the nose up   lashes, brows, hair   the read is feminine. From the nose down   jaw, throat, stubble shadow   the read is male and the nose itself is neutral territory, the DMZ between two interpretations of the same face.

From the nose down. Camera positioned to show from the nose down. Or from the nose up. Different framings for different truths.

From the nose up.

<<lina "The wig comes with a care kit. It's yours   our welcome gift. We hope to see you again soon, Ellie.">>

The way she says "soon" has the same certainty as "we'll see you soon" at the end of a first dealer's sample. Not if. When.

<<eli "Thanks.">>

<<lina "Of course. Dr. Voss wanted me to mention   your Dollars credit is active. The brow sculpt balance is due within seven days and if you'd like to explore Tier 1 further... the full menu is available through our app.">>

An app. Of course there's an app. Of course the bioengineering clinic that makes you prettier has a mobile application for browsing the catalog and scheduling procedures and managing your transformation like a subscription service. Of course.

<<eli "I'll think about it.">>

<<lina "Of course. We'll see you soon, Ellie.">>

<<lina "Take your time. The door is always open.">>

She says it with the certainty of a person who knows the difference between "goodbye" and "see you soon," who understands that the free eyelashes and the free wig are not charity but investment, and that the investment will pay off because it always does, because the door only opens one way.

[[Take of the wig and Leave Facility|day_3_10_other]]

:: day_3_10_other
<<set $hint to "Leave the clinic">>
<<set $playerImage = "images/user/tier_0_2.jpeg">>
I leave RegenaForm with a bag containing the wig and the care kit and the knowledge that my face is actively restructuring itself as I walk. The January air hits me   cold, clean, the kind that makes your newly enhanced eyelashes feel longer because the cold air moves against them differently. I can feel them. The lashes. A faint awareness of their presence, like wearing new jewelry you haven't adjusted to yet.
<<set $day_step to 10>>
Four blocks home. I walk fast, the bag swinging from my hand, head down, hood up. Nobody looks at me. Nobody has ever looked at me   I'm the guy people scroll past, the face that doesn't register   and right now the invisibility is a gift because I'm walking home from a clinic that changed my face and I need to know if the changes are visible.

I round the corner of my block and 

<<marc "Yo!">>

Marcus. Standing on the sidewalk in front of the laundromat, holding a bag of clean clothes, wearing the oversized charcoal hoodie and basketball shorts despite it being January, because Marcus dresses for the weather he wants, not the weather he has. His beat-up Jordans are untied. The scar on his left eyebrow catches the afternoon light.

<<marc "Where you coming from?">>

My heart rate spikes. The bag from RegenaForm suddenly weighs forty pounds. I shift it behind my leg.

<<eli "Just walking. Needed air.">>

<<marc "You don't go outside.">>

<<eli "I go outside.">>

<<marc "Name the last time.">>

<<eli "...yesterday. Kira and I ">>

<<marc "Before yesterday.">>

<<eli "I went to the convenience store last week.">>

<<marc "For Monster Energy.">>

<<eli "It was outside.">>

He looks at me. The Marcus look   direct, unhurried, the brown eyes that hold you and don't let go. He's scanning my face the way he always does, the quick visual check-in that he'd never admit is a welfare assessment but which is, objectively, a welfare assessment. He does it every time he sees me. Every time, for two years, Marcus has looked at my face and read it like a status report.

<<marc "You look different.">>

My stomach drops. The lashes. The brows. He sees them. He 

<<marc "You do something with your hair?">>

<<eli "Washed it.">>

<<marc "With what, actual shampoo?">>

<<eli "Hotel shampoo. Marc gave it to me.">>

<<marc "I know. I'm Marc.">>

<<eli "Right. Yeah.">>

He squints at me for another second. Then shrugs.

<<marc "Looks better. You should wash it more than once a year.">>

<<eli "Noted.">>

<<marc "Where you headed?">>

<<eli "Home. Streaming stuff.">>

<<marc "The mysterious streaming stuff.">>

<<eli "It's not mysterious. It's just... stuff.">>

<<marc "You and Kira both say that. 'Stuff.' Like it's classified.">>

<<eli "It's not classified. It's boring.">>

<<marc "Nothing about you is boring, bro. Tragic, maybe. Not boring.">>

He adjusts the laundry bag on his shoulder. The conversation is casual   this is how we always talk, in the space between going somewhere and getting there, a running commentary that doesn't ask for depth but allows it. Marcus never forces the serious stuff. He just walks beside it and waits.

<<marc "You talk to Kira today?">>

<<eli "Yeah. Briefly.">>

<<marc "How's that going? After the... thing?">>

The thing. The moment. The "it's hot" and the laugh and the taking it back.

<<eli "Fine. Normal.">>

<<marc "Normal.">>

<<eli "We're friends, Marc.">>

<<marc "Mhm.">>

<<eli "Don't mhm me.">>

<<marc "You called her hot and then said 'just kidding' like someone defusing a bomb they built themselves, and now you're telling me it's 'normal.' That's not normal. That's emotional bomb disposal.">>

<<eli "It was a joke.">>

<<marc "It wasn't.">>

<<eli "Marc ">>

<<marc "I'm just saying. You had the moment. You bailed and now you're doing 'streaming stuff' on a Thursday afternoon instead of talking to her about it.">>

He's right. He's always right. Marcus has this infuriating quality of being correct about emotional situations he's observing from the outside, like a sports commentator who can see plays developing before the players can. He saw the Kira thing coming. He called it and he's watching me fumble the aftermath with the same mixture of affection and exasperation that defines our entire friendship.

<<eli "I'll figure it out.">>

<<marc "You're not going to figure it out. You're going to avoid it until it becomes impossible to avoid and then panic. That's your pattern.">>

<<eli "I don't have a pattern.">>

<<marc "You have the most consistent pattern of anyone I know. Step one: identify problem. Step two: acknowledge problem. Step three: do absolutely nothing about problem until problem becomes crisis. Step four: panic.">>

<<eli "That's... uncomfortably accurate.">>

<<marc "I've had two years of data.">>

We stand there on the sidewalk. The January wind cuts between the buildings. My face is changing beneath the skin   the brows still reshaping, the cells still restructuring   and Marcus is two feet away and he doesn't see it. He looked at my face, the face he's known for years, and he noticed the hair and nothing else.

The lashes, the brows   they're invisible to him. Not invisible objectively   they're there, they're physical, they're real   but invisible socially. The changes are subtle enough that a person who knows my face reads them as "he washed his hair" rather than "he went to a bioengineering clinic and had his eyelashes modified and his brows restructured."

The test passed. The first test. If Marcus   who has two years of data, who does welfare assessments with his eyes, who notices when I text at 4 AM about penguin knees   doesn't see it, then nobody will.

<<marc "Alright, I gotta drop this off and get to work. ScreenFixx doesn't fix itself.">>

<<eli "That's actually the business model.">>

<<marc "Ha. See you later and talk to Kira.">>

<<eli "I'll think about it.">>

<<marc "You'll avoid it. But I said my piece.">>

He walks off. The laundry bag bounces against his back. His untied Jordans slap the pavement. I watch him go   my best friend, my welfare assessor, the person who tells me truths I don't want to hear   and I feel the weight of the bag in my hand. The wig. The care kit. The evidence of a choice he doesn't know about, in a bag he didn't ask about, behind the leg he wasn't looking at.

I walk the last block home.

The apartment. The door. The key. The hallway that smells like cleaning products and someone's dinner. The stairs (elevator still sounds like a submarine, still don't trust it). The door to my unit. The lock. The interior.

Same apartment. Same crack. Same everything.

Except me.

I set the bag on the desk. Open it. The wig sits inside, nestled in tissue paper, the brownish-auburn waves catching the overhead light (flicker, flicker, on) like something alive. I touch it. The fiber is soft   softer than my real hair, softer than anything in this apartment, which is a low bar but still.

I place it on the desk beside the monitors. It sits there like a prop waiting for its scene.

<<link "Go Home and Stream">><<set $hint to "Go Home and Stream">><<goto neighbourhood>><</link>>









:: day_3_11_other
<<set $playerImage to "images/user/tier_0_3.jpeg">>
8:00 PM. The apartment is dark except for the monitors. I've spent the last three hours in a state I can only describe as preparation   not the productive kind, not the kind that results in a clean desk or an organized scene layout, but the internal kind. The psychological loading screen. The process of deciding to do something you've already decided to do.

The cold one: ElliePlayz. The dashboard. The $47. The 89 viewers. The clinic. The wig in the closet.

Both real. Both mine. Both impossible to hold simultaneously without something cracking.

I turn on the light. The bulb flickers twice. Everything appears. The mattress, the desk, the monitors, the fridge, the burrito wrappers (two now, a growing collection). The closet with the wig buried in it.

I sit at the desk. Open the closet. Pull the wig out. Set it on the desk beside the monitors.

It sits there   brownish-auburn, soft waves, the StreamLine series from RegenaForm, designed for camera use   and it looks like a prop. A costume piece. A thing that belongs to someone else, waiting to be put on.

I open OBS. Open VoxMorph. Open the ElliePlayz channel. The dashboard from this afternoon's stream is still visible:

Peak Viewers: 89
Average Viewers: 63
Followers: 51
Donations: $47.00

51 followers. Some of them said they'd be back tonight. MidnightWhale99 said "tonight. I'll be there." ChillVibesOnly said "best new streamer on Twitch." People are waiting. Strangers who've never seen my face are waiting for a voice that doesn't exist to come back.

I put on the wig.

The process takes ten minutes   I watched a YouTube tutorial on the walk home, pausing at streetlights, memorizing the steps. Tuck the natural hair. Position the cap. Align the lace front. Secure with adhesive strips. The wig settles onto my head with a weight that's both physical and metaphorical   the feeling of putting on a costume, of stepping into a role, of becoming someone who isn't you and is entirely you.

I look at the webcam preview in OBS. The image is 

Different. Completely different.

The wig changes everything. The brownish-auburn waves frame the face in a way my greasy brown hair never did   soft, warm, feminine. The lashes catch the monitor light and cast those tiny shadows. The brows, now almost fully reshaped, lift the eye area into something open and inviting. The combination   wig, lashes, brows   doesn't make me look female. Not yet. The jaw is still too angular, the nose too wide, the skin too rough. But from certain angles, in certain light, with certain framing 

I adjust the webcam. Tilt it down. Frame the shot from the nose above. eyes, eyelashes, eye brows,forehead and the cascade of wig-hair falling from the top of my head. I angle my desk lamp to the side, creating a warm, directional light that softens the jaw shadow and makes the skin look smoother than it is.

In the preview, from the nose up without nose visibility, in this lighting, with this wig, I look like 

I look like a girl.

Not a beautiful girl. Not a convincing girl. But a plausible one. A girl who's shy about showing her face. A girl who streams with the camera low because she's nervous. A girl named Ellie who has a cute voice and 51 followers and whose full face reveal is always "coming eventually."

I turn on VoxMorph. Route the mic through OBS. Test the voice.

<<chat "ElliePlayz" "Hey everyone, welcome back.">>

The voice comes through the monitoring feed   feminine, warm, the same voice from this afternoon   and combined with the visual of the wig and the chin and the lips in the webcam preview, it's... convincing. Not flawless. Not indistinguishable. But convincing the way a good magic trick is convincing   you know it's not real, but if you don't look too hard, the illusion holds.

I check the time. 11:00 PM. Late-night Twitch. The same time slot that worked this afternoon (which was actually early afternoon, different vibe, but the late-night slot is the one the guide recommended for new streamers).

I change the OBS scene. Add the webcam   the nose-up framing without even nose visibility, the warm lighting. The ElliePlayz overlay, the pink-purple gradient, the cursive text. The game capture. Chat.

I hover over "Go Live."

[[Go Live|day_3_12_other]]

:: day_3_12_other
