[[End Stream|day_3_27_other]]

:: day_3_27_other
<<set $day_step to 43>>
<<set $playerImage to "images/user/tier_0_2.jpeg">>
I hit End Stream. VoxMorph stops. The monitoring feed goes silent and the room fills with the sound of my real breathing   low, male, the sound that doesn't earn $125 in a night.
<<set $right_sidebar_mode to 0>>
The dashboard sits there. The numbers sit there.

Duration: 2 hours 14 minutes
Peak Viewers: 231
Average Viewers: 142
New Followers: 97
Total Followers: 148
Donations: $163.00
Subscribers: 6

Combined ElliePlayz earnings: $210.00 (Session 1: $47 + Session 2: $163)

I close OBS. Close VoxMorph. Take off the wig. Place it on the care form beside the desk. The brownish-auburn waves settle into stillness. Without the wig, my face reappears in the dead webcam preview   the face beneath the costume, the person beneath the performance. Pale. Tired. Male. The new lashes look strange without the wig and the framing   feminine accessories on a masculine canvas, conspicuous in the wrong context, invisible in the right one. The reshaped brows arch softly over tired eyes that no one is calling amber or golden anymore. Just brown. Just dull. Just Eli.

I check my phone. Two messages.

<<kira "hey hope you're doing ok. you seemed quiet today. eat something real tonight, not a burrito. i worry about you. ❤️">>

<<marc "yo we still on for rift walkers this weekend? also you left your controller charger at my place">>

I read Kira's message twice. The red heart. She's still sending them   even after I laughed off the moment, even after I closed the door, even after the "it's hot" died in the emergency chuckle and neither of us has mentioned it since. She still worries. She still checks. The warmth is there, banked and patient, the warmth of a person who decided to care about me and hasn't uncared yet.

I don't send a heart back. I send what I always send.

<<eli "ate something. thanks kira. night">>

Bare. Two sentences and a period. The emotional equivalent of a nod across a room.

<<eli "yeah this weekend works. i'll grab the charger">>

I put the phone down. Lie on the mattress. Grey sheets. Thin comforter. Ceiling crack.

The apartment is quiet in a way that Kira's apartment never is   no cat purring on an armrest, no hum of a full-sized refrigerator with actual food in it, no residual warmth of another person's presence in the cushions. Just the mini-fridge. Just the pipes. Just the sound of a building settling into its own decay and a person settling into his.

The numbers float in the dark behind my closed eyes:

EliPlays : $3.50. Peak viewers: 5. Followers: 54.
ElliePlayz : $210.00 (combined sessions). Peak viewers: 231. Followers: 148.

The gap. The gap isn't between me and Kira anymore. It isn't between me and the female streamers on the browse page. It's between me and myself. Between the person I am in the morning and the person I become at night. Between the stream nobody watches and the stream everybody wants. Between the name in the Discord message   Eli   and the name on the dashboard   Ellie and beneath those numbers, another thought   the one that arrived during the stream and refused to leave. The face reveal. The crop line. The jaw and the lips and the nose still waiting below the frame, masculine and visible, the border between Ellie's world and Eli's reality. The thought that whispered: unless the face changed more.

RegenaForm's app is on my phone. I didn't open it. I don't open it now.

But I know it's there.

[[Stare at the ceiling crack|day_3_28_other]]

:: day_3_28_other
<<set $day_step to 28>>
<<set $day to 3>>
I stare at the crack. It's growing. Everything is growing   the crack, the followers, the donations, the lie, the guilt, the thought of what comes next. Everything is growing and nothing is stopping and the door that opened today opens only one way and I'm through it now, standing in a room I can't leave, wearing a face that isn't mine and isn't not-mine, holding a name that is two letters from my own.

Ellie.

Eli.

The same person. Different key.

But the key works. That's the thing my brain keeps circling back to, the fact it can't release, the number it can't stop chewing on. The key works. $210 today. From half a face. From eyes and lashes and brows and hair   from the nose up, from the feminized slice, from the fraction I'm willing to show.

Half a face earned $210. Not even half a face, really. A quarter of a face, if you think about it. The eyes and hair, the part that reads feminine, the part that performs. The part that makes people say "those eyes could END me." The part that makes people click and stay and donate and simper and build a community around a person who doesn't exist.

What would the full face earn?

The math does itself. It runs unbidden, the way the math always runs in my head at this hour   not requested, not welcome, just there, a calculator that never sleeps, tabulating in the dark while I stare at the crack. If half the face   the pretty half, the modified half, the half that stops scrolls and earns donations and makes strangers say "those eyes could END me"   produces $210 in two sessions... what would the whole face produce? The full reveal. The eyes AND the lips AND the jaw AND the chin. The complete Ellie, not the cropped version, not the suggestion, but the person entire.

Double? Triple? The face reveal is the most anticipated event on the channel   chat talks about it in stream, the curiosity building like pressure in a sealed container. When it finally happens, the viewer spike alone could push me past 500. Past 1,000. The donations during a face reveal stream would be   I can see it, I can see the number, and it's not $210. It's $500. It's $1,000. It's the kind of number that changes a bank account from a countdown to a foundation.

But the full face can't happen yet. The jaw. The lips. The nose. The masculine tells that live below the crop line, waiting to betray me. A few more modifications   that's all. Not a full transformation. Not the deep tiers. Just... finishing what I started. Jawline softening. Lip enhancement. Nose refinement. Tier 1 stuff. The same tier I'm already in. Just completing the set. Filling in the gaps between the eyes and the chin until the whole face reads the way the upper half already does.

A few more changes. That's all. A few more changes to show the full face and it wouldn't hurt anyone, I can always hide it with some minor changes. Without wig all these changes not make much difference. Wig is the one connnecting all nodes into one and make it complete.

That's the part that locks into place like a deadbolt   the rationalization that seals the others shut, the capstone on the wall between what I'm doing and what it means. It wouldn't hurt anyone. The viewers are entertained. They're choosing to be here. The donations are voluntary. The parasocial bond is the same bond every streamer has with every audience   Kira has it with her chat, every Just Chatting streamer has it, every face on Twitch has it. I'm not scamming anyone. I'm performing. I'm providing a service. The fact that the performer doesn't match the performance is   what? Theater. Drag. Character work. The internet runs on personas. Nobody's real name is their username. Nobody's stream personality is their actual personality. I'm just taking it one step further. One step further with bioengineering and voice modulation and a wig.

A few more changes to the face won't hurt a single soul on this earth. The viewers get their content. The algorithm gets its engagement. RegenaForm gets its StreamCoin and I get a life and the money   the money isn't just for me. That's what makes the math noble instead of desperate. That's the thread I pull on until the whole rationalization unspools into something that looks almost like virtue.

I could stop being a weight.

Marcus. Every coffee he buys me without mentioning it. Every time he covers lunch and changes the subject before I can offer to split. Every cheese puff and every "come thru if u want" and every shoulder punch that's really a welfare check disguised as friendship. He's been carrying me for two years   not financially, not exactly, but emotionally, practically, the way a person carries a friend who's drowning by keeping them near the surface without ever acknowledging the water. I've been the friend who can't buy his own coffee. The friend whose bank account is a punchline. The friend who takes and takes and calls it friendship because the alternative is admitting he's a burden.

$210 in one day. If this continues   if the numbers hold, if the growth sustains   I can pay Marcus back. Not literally. He'd never accept money and he'd be insulted if I offered. But I can stop being the friend who needs covering. I can buy my own coffee. I can bring the snacks to his apartment instead of eating his. I can show up with a Tupperware of something that isn't a microwave burrito and say "I made this" and he'd look at me sideways and say "since when do you cook" and I'd say "since I could afford groceries" and neither of us would need to acknowledge what changed because the change would be visible in the Tupperware and the groceries and the fact that I'm standing in his kitchen with food I bought with money I earned.

Kira. The friend who buys me ramen and drives me places and sends red hearts I don't return. The friend who told me the truth about the system and then watched me fail to use the information. Except I am using it now. She just doesn't know.

If ElliePlayz works, I could tell her. Maybe. Eventually. I can picture the conversation   sitting in her apartment, Packet on the armrest judging us both, and me saying: "Remember when you told me the system rewards a specific input? I found a way to change the input." nd she'd look at me with those analytical eyes, the ones that see everything, and she'd understand. Because Kira understands systems. She identified the problem. She'd respect the solution, even if the solution is unconventional, even if it involves a wig and a voice modulator and a clinic four blocks from my apartment.

She'd understand. She might even be impressed. The pragmatist in her   the part that writes essays about parasocial economics for fun   would see the elegance of it. The same system that punished Eli rewards Ellie. Same person. Different packaging. Market arbitrage.

Marc would understand too. Eventually. Once there's something to show   once the numbers are undeniable, once the income is real and sustained, once I'm not presenting a theory but a result. Marc doesn't care about methods. He cares about outcomes. He cares about whether I'm okay. If the outcome of a wig and some facial modifications and a fake name is that I'm okay   actually, financially, measurably okay   he'd nod and say "wild" and ask if I'd eaten today and the conversation would move on because that's what Marcus does. He absorbs. He adjusts. He carries on.

I'll tell them both. When the time is right. When the face is further along. When the numbers are big enough that the confession comes with evidence attached.

My mom.

God. My mom.

My mom who works two jobs in another state   one at a grocery store, one doing night cleaning at an office building   and sends money she can't afford through Venmo every other month with texts that say "hope you're eating ok." Four words. The maximum emotional bandwidth of a woman who loves her son but doesn't know how to say it and doesn't have the energy to learn because both of her energies   the first shift and the second shift   are spent by the time she gets home to an apartment that's probably not much bigger than mine.

She sends me $1500 every two months. $1500 she earns standing on her feet for eight hours scanning groceries and then standing on her feet for four more hours pushing a vacuum through cubicle farms. $1500 that crosses the distance between her state and mine and lands in my Venmo like a care package from a war zone, except the war zone is just America and the casualties are just dignity.

$210. I earned $210 today. In one day. Sitting in a chair. Talking into a camera. Playing a video game.

My mother earns that in two months of supplemental labor on top of two jobs.

If ElliePlayz works   if the money continues, if the growth holds   I can stop taking her money. I can send her money. I can open Venmo and reverse the arrow and type "hope YOU'RE eating ok" and attach a number that's big enough to matter and she'd see it on her phone between shifts and she might cry or she might just nod the way she nods at things she doesn't fully understand but recognizes as good.

I could call her. Pick up the phone and video call and when she sees my face   whatever face it is by then, whatever configuration the modifications have produced   she'd see a person who's okay. Not the gaunt, pale, acne-marked, dark-circled face she's afraid to see. A face that has color. That has rest. That has the specific luminosity of a person who isn't drowning anymore.

She doesn't need to know how the money was earned. She doesn't need to know about Ellie or RegenaForm or the voice modulator. She just needs to know her son is okay. If the path to okay runs through a wig and a webcam and a name that's two letters different from his own   that's a path. That's more path than he's had in eight months. That's more path than the ceiling crack and the microwave burritos and the $12.50 monthly income offered.

The brow sculpt cost $300. Today I earned $210. Two-thirds of the cost covered in a single day. Tomorrow, another stream. Another $100, $150, $200. By the end of the week, the brow sculpt is paid off. By the end of next week, I can afford the next modification.

Nose refinement   $500. Then lips   $400. Then jawline   $600 to $900. Each one dropping the crop line lower. Each one bringing the face reveal closer. Each one feeding the loop that feeds the income that feeds the life that feeds the people who've been feeding me.

The math works. The money is real. The face is changing. The audience is growing.

A few more changes. A few more trips to Suite 12. A few more sessions in the chair with the cool gel and the warmth and the cells restructuring beneath the skin.

It won't hurt anyone. The viewers get entertainment. RegenaForm gets paid. Marc gets a friend who can buy coffee. Kira gets a friend who figured it out. Mom gets a son who's okay and I get a life.

The ceiling crack is still there. Still growing. But it's sharing the ceiling now with numbers that also grow   numbers that go up instead of apart, numbers that build instead of break.

I close my eyes. The fridge hums. The wig sits on its form in the closet, patient, waiting for tomorrow. The RegenaForm app glows on my phone beside the mattress, a small teal icon, the double helix in miniature, a door in my pocket that opens with a tap.

A whole face is coming. When it arrives   when Ellie's face is complete and the crop line drops to nothing and the stream goes live with everything visible   the number won't be $210.

It'll be enough.

Enough to stop being a weight. Enough to buy coffee. Enough to bring snacks. Enough to call my mom.

I sleep and for the first time, I don't dream about the crack splitting wider. I dream about it filling in   plaster smoothing over the fault line, the ceiling repairing itself, the apartment becoming a place instead of a sentence.

I dream about sending money instead of spending trust.

I dream about a face that isn't finished yet, but will be. Soon.

[[Sleep|eli_bed]]
